{
  "case_id": "2006-05-608",
  "document_hash": "797774137e8c3cf1953e98afb88a76a882d4c2d1de71f44646954ade4048a94e",
  "records": [
    {
      "attribution": "John G. Kester (Petitioner)",
      "case_id": "2006-05-608",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "797774137e8c3cf1953e98afb88a76a882d4c2d1de71f44646954ade4048a94e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-797774137e8c-0000-0000",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 3,
        "start": 3
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Mr. Kester: Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: As of this morning, it is exactly 70 years ago to the day, minus 4 months, that this Court heard argument challenging the new Federal Declaratory Judgment Act of 1934, in an action to construe an insurance contract. And exactly 25 years -- 25 days later, in a unanimous opinion written by Chief Justice Hughes, joined by Justices Stone, Brandeis, and others, the Act was held fully consistent with Article III of the Constitution. This morning, you are here because an action was brought for a declaratory judgment that a biomedical manufacturer need not play -- pay large sums, under a license as patent royalties, under a patent it contends is invalid, unenforceable, and not infringed, but is paying royalties under protest in the meantime."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Kennedy",
      "case_id": "2006-05-608",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "797774137e8c3cf1953e98afb88a76a882d4c2d1de71f44646954ade4048a94e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-797774137e8c-0000-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 5,
        "start": 5
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE KENNEDY: Well, but as a matter of policy, we, at some point, either in this case or some later case, may have to address the question of whether or not such a provision is enforceable."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Dean E. Maynard (Amicus Curiae for United States)",
      "case_id": "2006-05-608",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "797774137e8c3cf1953e98afb88a76a882d4c2d1de71f44646954ade4048a94e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-797774137e8c-0000-0001",
      "kind": "amicus_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 17,
        "start": 17
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. MAYNARD: Mr. Chief Justice, and may it please the Court: There is a concrete dispute between the parties about their legal rights and obligations. If that dispute is resolved, money will change hands. That is an Article III case or controversy."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice BREYER",
      "case_id": "2006-05-608",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "797774137e8c3cf1953e98afb88a76a882d4c2d1de71f44646954ade4048a94e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-797774137e8c-0001-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 20,
        "start": 20
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Now, is -- if -- I guess there are three possible positions on the question of whether a licensee can attack a contract, a patent where he has a license and wants to keep the contract. One, he can never do it. Two, he can always do it. Three, it depends on what the contract says. Now, do any of those questions have anything to do with the question before us, which is whether it is a case or controversy?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice SCALIA",
      "case_id": "2006-05-608",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "797774137e8c3cf1953e98afb88a76a882d4c2d1de71f44646954ade4048a94e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-797774137e8c-0001-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 22,
        "start": 22
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Contractually? They say that that's their contractual right?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice SCALIA",
      "case_id": "2006-05-608",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "797774137e8c3cf1953e98afb88a76a882d4c2d1de71f44646954ade4048a94e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-797774137e8c-0001-0002",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 23,
        "start": 23
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "Where does that appear in the licensing agreement? Or --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. MAYNARD",
      "case_id": "2006-05-608",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "797774137e8c3cf1953e98afb88a76a882d4c2d1de71f44646954ade4048a94e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-797774137e8c-0001-0008",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 24,
        "start": 24
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "It's also -- the licensee also does not need to breach the licensing agreement in order to create a case or controversy. The licensee is currently paying royalties that it does not believe it owes and that it believes it would be entitled to have back if it should prevail on its interpretation of the -- of the patent and the licensing agreement."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. MAHONEY",
      "case_id": "2006-05-608",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "797774137e8c3cf1953e98afb88a76a882d4c2d1de71f44646954ade4048a94e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-797774137e8c-0001-0010",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 30,
        "start": 30
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "They can't just show up here today and say, \"Well, there really is a dispute about the contract.\" But they never argued, below, and is foreclosed --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. MAHONEY",
      "case_id": "2006-05-608",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "797774137e8c3cf1953e98afb88a76a882d4c2d1de71f44646954ade4048a94e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-797774137e8c-0001-0011",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 34,
        "start": 34
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "It's a compromise. It's a compromise of the very claims they're trying to adjudicate here."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2006-05-608",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "797774137e8c3cf1953e98afb88a76a882d4c2d1de71f44646954ade4048a94e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-797774137e8c-0002-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 37,
        "start": 37
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE STEVENS: -- whether the parties could agree to create a case or controversy."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. MAHONEY",
      "case_id": "2006-05-608",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "797774137e8c3cf1953e98afb88a76a882d4c2d1de71f44646954ade4048a94e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-797774137e8c-0002-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 44,
        "start": 44
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. MAHONEY: -- but I think, here, that the major problem, from a jurisdictional perspective, is that there is not anything in the language of the contract that gives them a right to come to court to dispute validity. Instead, we're --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Ms. MAHONEY",
      "case_id": "2006-05-608",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "797774137e8c3cf1953e98afb88a76a882d4c2d1de71f44646954ade4048a94e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-797774137e8c-0002-0003",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 45,
        "start": 45
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "MS. MAHONEY: I don't think that the patent holder is allowed to come to court and seek a declaration of validity. I don't think any court has ever allowed that."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Ginsburg",
      "case_id": "2006-05-608",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "797774137e8c3cf1953e98afb88a76a882d4c2d1de71f44646954ade4048a94e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-797774137e8c-0003-0000",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 55,
        "start": 55
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE GINSBURG: But what -- but jurisdiction is a question of power, Does the Court have the power to do this? A discretion question is different. It's, \"We have the power to entertain this case, but, as a matter of equity, we're not going to do so.\" The power question, I think, is a -- one that's -- it's either yes or no, either the court has the power, or doesn't."
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Stevens",
      "case_id": "2006-05-608",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "797774137e8c3cf1953e98afb88a76a882d4c2d1de71f44646954ade4048a94e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-797774137e8c-0003-0001",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 56,
        "start": 56
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE STEVENS: Ms. Mahoney, can I ask you one question before your light goes off? I know it's not -- goes to the \"case or controversy\" issue, but, in your view, was the bringing of this action a material breach of an implied condition of the contract that would justify a termination of a license?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "John G. Kester",
      "case_id": "2006-05-608",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "797774137e8c3cf1953e98afb88a76a882d4c2d1de71f44646954ade4048a94e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-797774137e8c-0003-0002",
      "kind": "party_argument",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 57,
        "start": 57
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "REBUTTAL ARGUMENT OF JOHN G. KESTER ON BEHALF OF PETITIONER MR. KESTER: Thank you, Mr. Chief Justice. Just several quick items. I think -- I think, Mr. Chief Justice, you were, a while ago, putting the horse in front of the cart, which was right where it belongs. The contract claim is clear in the record. It's at page 136 of the joint appendix. I don't think more needs to be said about it. Harvey Steel, on which Respondents rely, was, of course, overruled --"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Justice Scalia",
      "case_id": "2006-05-608",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "797774137e8c3cf1953e98afb88a76a882d4c2d1de71f44646954ade4048a94e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-797774137e8c-0003-0003",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 58,
        "start": 58
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "JUSTICE SCALIA: Wait, wait. Before you leave that, do you agree that it was not raised below?"
    },
    {
      "attribution": "Chief Justice Roberts",
      "case_id": "2006-05-608",
      "confidence": 1.0,
      "document_hash": "797774137e8c3cf1953e98afb88a76a882d4c2d1de71f44646954ade4048a94e",
      "evidence_id": "ev-797774137e8c-0003-0004",
      "kind": "justice_question",
      "opinion_part": null,
      "pages": {
        "end": 59,
        "start": 59
      },
      "schema_version": "1.0.0",
      "status": "supported",
      "text": "CHIEF JUSTICE ROBERTS: The case is submitted."
    },
